 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training throughput is accelerated by three orders of magnitude compared to vanilla NeRF due to efficient hash-table lookups and interpolation operations [S1]. Rendering is real-time capable, suitable for virtual anchors and video conferencing [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the resolution of the neural feature map and the size of $K$ [G]. Explicit mesh storage adds overhead compared to implicit fields but allows for efficient culling and rasterization [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability; efficient training; explicit geometry allows for easy integration with standard graphics pipelines (e.g., differentiable rasterization) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires an explicit mesh and UV parameterization; quality depends on the accuracy of the mesh and the expressiveness of the feature map [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time applications, virtual production, and scenarios requiring explicit geometry control [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with complex, unstructured geometry where meshing is difficult or expensive [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   The architecture achieves a three-order-of-magnitude acceleration in training throughput compared to vanilla NeRF due to efficient hash-table lookups and interpolation operations [S1].
+*   Memory consumption is lower than Plenoxels, which discretizes the scene into a sparse voxel grid and optimizes spherical harmonic coefficients directly, albeit at the cost of increased memory [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Produces standard physically based textures compatible with traditional renderers; enables real-time animation for virtual humans; supports deferred rendering workflows [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the explicit 3D mesh proxy; may suffer from artifacts if the mesh is inaccurate or poorly UV-unwrapped [S1].
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   Use when explicit geometry is available or can be approximated well by a mesh.
+*   Use when compatibility with traditional graphics pipelines is required.
+*   Use for real-time applications like virtual avatars or digital humans [S1].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+*   Use implicit methods when geometry is complex, unbounded, or difficult to represent with a single mesh.
+*   Use Plenoxels when memory is not a constraint and higher quality without deep neural networks is desired [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Neural Textures was introduced as a method to extend traditional 2D texture maps into high-dimensional feature spaces associated with explicit 3D meshes [S1]. It emerged from the need for real-time neural rendering, bridging the gap between traditional rasterization and implicit neural fields [S1]. The approach leverages differentiable rasterization to make the feature query and coloring process end-to-end differentiable [S1]. Variants using SIRENs have been explored for neural lumigraph rendering to handle view-dependent effects and angular smoothness [S3]. Applications include virtual digital humans, virtual production (e.g., LED Volumes), and special effects [S1].
+Neural Textures Architecture extends the concept of traditional 2D texture mapping into the neural rendering domain. It was introduced as part of the broader exploration of explicit representations in neural rendering, offering a hybrid approach between explicit mesh-based rendering and implicit neural fields [S1]. The method leverages differentiable rasterization to bridge the gap between standard graphics pipelines and neural optimization [S1].
 
